#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bessel_y

#% text
# `bessel_y(nu, x)` gives the Bessel function of the second kind `Y_nu(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `Y_nu(x)` is the solution of Bessel's equation that is singular at 0 (DLMF 10.2.3), defined for `x > 0`; it oscillates with decreasing amplitude for large `x`.

#% item
# Half-integer orders give closed forms in `sin` and `cos`; real machine numbers and balls give numerical values for integer and fractional orders.

#% item
# `bessel_y` is listable in both arguments.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
bessel_y(0, 1.0)
#% output
#= 0.08825696421567696

#% cell
bessel_y(1, 2.5)
#% output
#= 0.1459181379667858

#% cell
bessel_y(1/2, x)
#% output
#= -cos(x)*2^(1/2)/(pi*x)^(1/2)

#% cell
n(bessel_y(0, 1), 30)
#% output
#= 0.08825696421567695798292676602351516

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A fractional order:

#% cell
bessel_y(1.5, 3.0)
#% output
#= 0.08700809072083528

#% text
# Large arguments:

#% cell
bessel_y(0, 1000.0)
#% output
#= 0.0047159179776228135

#% text
# Several orders:

#% cell
bessel_y([0, 1, 2], 1.0)
#% output
#= [0.08825696421567696, -0.7812128213002887, -1.6506826068162543]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The first positive zero of `Y_0`:

#% cell
find_root(bessel_y(0, x) == 0, [x, 0.9])
#% output
#= [x -> 0.8935769662791675]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The Wronskian `J_1 Y_0 - J_0 Y_1 = 2/(pi x)`:

#% cell
bessel_j(1, 2.0)*bessel_y(0, 2.0) - bessel_j(0, 2.0)*bessel_y(1, 2.0) - 2/(pi*2.0)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The function is singular at 0:

#% cell
bessel_y(0, 0)
#% output
#! failure(pole, bessel_y(0, 0))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `bessel_j`, `bessel_i`, `bessel_k`, `spherical_hankel_h1`
